{
  "notes": [
    "This is the forms run after Step 2.9's trees stage: run/attack-trees/ holds each tree as assemble-report.py trees leaves it (a copy of the baseline's file for a carried_forward item, the plan's tree otherwise) and the merged manifest. The report plan pins threats.md, the trees plan and that manifest by digest, and params.reference_digest is the run's reference set's. Eight batches, in the template's order: six section parts (executive summary, architecture overview, threat categories, cross-cutting themes, agentic patterns and the delta summary) and two findings parts (the threat analysis and the remediation roadmap).",
    "The run has more than 30 open findings, so each Medium, Low and Note finding is summarized by its category and the Critical and High findings are narrated one by one. No attack_chains part is planned: threats.md holds no outcome record, so the planner plans none and Section 6 would print the outcome line (S1P-A58). Each findings part fits in one batch (22 narrated findings and 144 roadmap rows are the sizes), so none is split.",
    "Two fragments are wrong on purpose. architecture_overview's trust_boundaries holds a digit outside a slot, so it takes the fixed fallback sentence (one narrative_fallback). exec_summary's risk_posture is longer than its 1,500 characters, and is cut where plain text stands (one truncation). Both parts are valid: a narrative that breaks the rules takes its fixed sentence and an over-limit one is cut, so reconcile accepts them (D3), and the report stage's record counts both.",
    "A section unit holds its section and key, and a category's unit its narrated and summarized IDs; a findings unit holds the finding's Section 7 row (a roadmap primary also its group and the members' rows). The contract says more rows go in a section unit, and no script reads them yet: T109's plan is what settles them."
  ],
